;; talk/kernel--protocol.scm — objections to kernel.scm and apps.scm, lens: protocol.

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-1 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'k-evidence :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/watchtower/last-resort-appointment.ts"
    "The capability reports coverage as 'covered, last accepted or failed', and the default sends a copy after every change. The protocol's appointment is bound to one exact signed state (one proof nonce, one appointment sequence) and is authorised by the owner's own signature. Every later state that both sides sign makes the stored appointment stale until the device builds and uploads a new one. A person who pays, receives or trades and then loses the phone before the upload has a tower holding an older state, while the kernel still says 'covered' and 'accepted'. The tower may also act only inside the final last-resort window, never earlier, and never starts a dispute. It is a trusted agent whose extra arguments (secrets, fills) are not bound by the owner's signature. So 'covered' promises more than the protocol gives: what the person needs to know is whether the newest signed state is the one held and whether the window is still open. As written, a person on an active account sees 'covered' most of the time and loses money in the one case it was shown for."))

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-2 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'k-leaving :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "jurisdictions/contracts/Depository.sol"
    "The screen answers 'how much of it is certain' from my locked share and what is owed to me with nothing locked, and shows nothing about what I owe. When an account is closed on the chain, a debt that goes beyond the collateral is paid first out of the debtor's own money on the chain, and the rest is recorded and takes every later arrival of the same token. A person who has used credit the other side granted, or who borrowed, will see a screen about getting money back while leaving takes money from their chain balance and from what comes later. Nothing on it says so. The owed-by-me figures exist in the ground and are not among the figures shown. A person reading this before the 'leave' question believes leaving can only return money, and it can also cost them."))

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-3 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'k-leaving-sums :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'keep-evidence
    "The capability states 'what leaving alone would return for sure' as a figure worked out from the current state, with no condition attached. The protocol makes my share certain only if I hold the latest state both sides signed and answer in time: a dispute can be answered only with a newer signed state, and the tower acts only in the final window. If my evidence is missing, stale or my device is gone, the same figure is the amount I could lose, yet it reads the same in every face. Nothing in the capability's definition ties the figure to whether the evidence is held, so the word 'sure' is printed by something that cannot know whether it is true. The cost is a safety claim on exactly the account where the person is in trouble."))

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-4 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'app-borrowing :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/protocol/dispute/lending-covenant.ts"
    "The app says the debt 'appears in the person's own face as money they owe with nothing locked', next to the same figures the kernel derives from the account's balance. A loan is a separate signed covenant held beside the balance, with its own principal, rate, term and late window. On leaving, the covenant is accelerated: principal plus interest earned so far is claimed at once, and the quote is frozen at the start of the dispute. That amount is not in the owed-by-me figures of the balance, so the holdings view, the leaving screen and what leaving would cost all omit it. A borrower who leaves a silent hub learns the size of the claim only after it is fixed. The app promises a place where the debt is visible, and the figure it points to does not contain it."))

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-5 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'app-trading-program :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'swap-cancel-ask-only
    "The app says the trader 'can stop it with one action'. Stopping the program stops new proposals only. Orders it already signed rest on the hub, hold the offered amount, and can be filled. Someone with an open swap order cannot cancel it alone; they can only ask the hub, and the amount stays held until the hub acts. With a hub that is slow or silent, the trader who pressed stop still has live orders at the prices the program chose, and the worst case is every open order filled. The limits that let orders be signed at once bound how much the program may propose, not what is already out. The app's description gives the trader a stop that the protocol does not provide."))

(objection 'o-kernel-protocol-6 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'k-leaving :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'exit-early-if-answered
    "The screen answers 'when does my money come back' and shows a single end time. The protocol lets that time move. The one who starts waits the whole window; if a newer state is registered on the chain, or a cross-chain swap leg is open on the account, both wait for the window and every swap deadline; and the counterparty may end it at once by accepting the state the starter put forward. The window itself is the sum of both sides' response times and the QA document says the rule may still change. A person who plans around the shown date (rent, a margin call) can be late by a day or more without any change on the screen."))
